 "id": "flyingv",
 "model": "Gibson Flying V, 1967-style (large one-piece '67 pickguard, pickups mounted in the guard, 3 knobs in a triangle + toggle + jack on the treble wing) on the 1958 V body outline; bridge modelled as Tune-o-matic + stopbar tailpiece (as on Gibson's Flying V 70s / most later '67-style Vs), NOT the '58 V-shaped string-through plate and NOT the '67 Maestro Vibrola",
 "sources": [
  {
   "url": "https://www.electricherald.com/diagrams/Gibson-Flying-V-1983.pdf",
   "what": "PRIMARY body: 1:1 vector '83 Flying V template (Wayback 20240423085354). Scale line nut->bridge 62.86 cm verified. Body outline (lines + beziers), '58-style hardware reference."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.electricherald.com/diagrams/gibson-flying-v/Gibson83FlyingV.pdf",
   "what": "Ian Alexander '83 Korina reissue drawing (raster, 1:1 at 50 px/in, measured from a real guitar): half-width 8 11/32 in, inner-edge angle 41.3 deg, wing tip R7/8, crotch R1 7/16, crotch->heel 11 7/32 in, posts->heel 7 27/32 in, body 1 1/4 in thick, neck angle 1.5 deg, headstock angle 17 deg. Used to verify the vector outline."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.electricherald.com/diagrams/gibson-flying-v/Gibson58FlyingV.pdf",
   "what": "1:1 vector '58 Flying V (nut 42.9 mm and 12th fret 31.43 cm verified): HEADSTOCK outline, tuner posts, bell truss-rod cover. Its body (45.0 cm wide) was rejected: photos and the '83 drawing give 41.9-42.6 cm."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.gibson.com/products/gibson-custom-1967-mahogany-flying-v-reissue-w-maestro-vibrola-gloss-sparkling-burgundy",
   "what": "Gibson Custom 1967 Flying V reissue product photos (FV67VOSBCM1_body/back): '67 pickguard shape (white guard segmented and line-fitted), knob/toggle/jack positions, '67 heel/shoulder shape, rear strap button. Calibrated on 6 fret lines (y) and TOM post spacing (x)."
  },
  {
   "url": "https://www.gibson.com/products/gibson-70s-flying-v-ebony",
